 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training iterations are standard 3DGS complexity, with additional overhead for sensitivity score accumulation (integrated into backward pass) and periodic densification/pruning [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the final Gaussian count $N_G$, which is automatically adjusted to scene complexity. TangoGS uses 48% fewer Gaussians than the best-performing baseline (LeGS) on standard benchmarks while matching PSNR [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** On 13 standard benchmark scenes, TangoGS matches the mean PSNR of LeGS with significantly fewer Gaussians. On 8 large captures, it automatically scales to larger models without retuning [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Single configuration across all 21 tested captures; no per-capture tuning required; robust to redundant views; compact models.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires sparse reconstruction track data for allowance calculation; sensitivity scoring relies on LiteGS kernel implementation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Adds minimal overhead to standard 3DGS training. Sensitivity scoring uses LiteGS's kernel, accumulating scores over an epoch without additional renders or gradient modifications [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires pre-allocated buffers for Morton-ordered groups of 128 elements to support efficient filtering and re-clustering [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** On 13 standard benchmark scenes, TangoGS matches the mean PSNR of the best-performing baseline (LeGS) with 48% fewer Gaussians [S1, unofficial]. On 8 large captures, it automatically scales to larger models without retuning [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Single configuration for all 21 tested captures; invariant to view redundancy; robust against catastrophic collapse via guards [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires accurate track-adjusted pixel counts from a sparse model; relies on PSNR as a proxy for quality, which may not capture all artifacts [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-TangoGS was introduced in the paper "Gauss What You Need: Compact Gaussian Splatting Across Scene Scales" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitation of standard 3DGS densification strategies that require manual tuning for different scene scales and capture densities. By decoupling model sizing (from capture metadata) from model refinement (from training quality), it provides a generalizable solution for compact Gaussian Splatting.
+TangoGS was introduced in the paper "Gauss What You Need: Compact Gaussian Splatting Across Scene Scales" [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Adaptive Density Control](9188-adaptive-density-control.md) and [Score-based Densification](4705-score-based-densification.md) but introduces a capture-derived budget to solve the problem of manual hyperparameter tuning across different scene scales. It is implemented using the LiteGS framework [S1, unofficial].
 
